WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.680
Why does the Crabfeeder's freaky mask look so&nbsp;
freakin' familiar? You're not imagining it —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.800
here's the surprising connection the villain's&nbsp;
headgear has to "Game of Thrones." Spoilers ahead!

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.880
HBO's highly anticipated prequel series, "House of&nbsp;
the Dragon," is pulling in massive numbers for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.160
network, proving that viewers are happy to return&nbsp;
to the fantasy world of Westeros , and in droves.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.560
One intriguing element of the show&nbsp;
thus far is its swift introduction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:32.400
and then just-as-swift killing off of the&nbsp;
villainous Crabfeeder, aka Craghas Drahar.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.040
"The Crabfeeder is packed by&nbsp;
powerful entities within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.134
Free Cities who wish to see Westeros weakened."

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.320
The masked, grayscale-afflicted baddie&nbsp;
served as the first major outside threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.000
to the Targaryen protagonists. However,&nbsp;
there's also something about him that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.600
surprisingly connects Crabfeeder&nbsp;
directly to "Game of Thrones."

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.680
In an interview with Entertainment Weekly,&nbsp;
Crabfeeder actor Daniel Scott-Smith talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.960
extensively about his time as the&nbsp;
character on "House of the Dragon."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.520
Among the more interesting tidbits was Scott-Smith&nbsp;
confirming a cool detail about his character's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.640
mask and its ties to a well-known "Game&nbsp;
of Thrones" cult: the Sons of the Harpy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.120
He told the outlet that he discussed the mask&nbsp;
with the director of Episodes 2 and 3, saying,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.760
"We spoke about the idea of him being the first&nbsp;
person to wear this mask and it becoming iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.760
and, therefore, it's built from that [for&nbsp;
'Game of Thrones']. Why is he wearing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.320
mask? How does he feel about that? It's a power&nbsp;
statement, so he's quite happy wearing it."

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.720
While this is a fascinating connection for&nbsp;
"House of the Dragon" to make to its parent show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.360
it obviously raises more questions than it&nbsp;
answers. What inspired the Sons of the Harpy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.200
to start wearing masks modeled after the one&nbsp;
that Crabfeeder wore? Is he something of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.840
legendary figure for them, or does it have&nbsp;
some deeper, perhaps religious connotations?

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.240
And perhaps most importantly of all, will&nbsp;
the showrunners dive into this topic further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.160
or will it be saved for some other,&nbsp;
far-off spin-off series? Regardless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.800
it's still a big connection between the shows&nbsp;
that diehard fans will no doubt appreciate.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.680
Daemon Targaryen kills Crabfeeder off-screen&nbsp;
in Episode 3, and the first serious outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.840
antagonist the family faces in "House of&nbsp;
the Dragon" is gone in the blink of an eye.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.520
Scott-Smith told EW that he never&nbsp;
even met Matt Smith on the set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.720
and that it was bizarre to see&nbsp;
his own prosthetic corpse on TV.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.480
"It's a strange experience [...] I don't think&nbsp;
many people would experience that normally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.720
but it was quite surreal. It was cool, though."

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.560
The Crabfeeder never speaks, but the&nbsp;
reappearance of the gold mask in "House&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.440
of the Dragon" helps visually signify that&nbsp;
this person is an enemy of the Targaryens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.520
just as the Sons of the Harpy were in "Game&nbsp;
of Thrones." Fans of the original series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.680
will remember the resistance group as one&nbsp;
of Daenerys Targaryen's foes in Seasons 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.000
and 6. The group forms to combat&nbsp;
Daenerys and her new rule of Meereen.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.280
The gold mask is a representation of The Harpy,&nbsp;
a gold figure with the head and torso of a woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:48.560
and the wings, tail, and legs of a bird. This was&nbsp;
a symbol of the slave masters in Slaver's Bay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.400
so why Crabfeeder would be wearing the&nbsp;
same mask is a bit of a question mark.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.960
His armies are from the free cities, so perhaps&nbsp;
this gold mask is just a way for "House of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:01.920
Dragon" to identify a foe of House Targaryen. Or,&nbsp;
perhaps, it was just something thrown in for "Game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.960
of Thrones" stans. Daniel Scott-Smith admitted&nbsp;
that was a factor in his EW interview, saying,

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.823
"It was definitely a nod to that for, I guess, the&nbsp;
fans, because it's something familiar for them."

